WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:21.500
Marius had lived in poverty for several years, but he had never known true desperation.

2
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:28.524
He had seen true desperation in the form of this girl—this phantom—who had just passed before his eyes.

3
00:00:29.464 --> 00:00:33.407
She had revealed to him an entirely new and hideous kind of poverty.

4
00:00:34.387 --> 00:00:40.171
She and her family had fallen so low, become so poor, that they could only be described as

5
00:00:40.291 --> 00:00:43.253
people living in misery—les misérables.

6
00:00:44.314 --> 00:00:49.838
Every day before her visit, Marius must have seen the girl, or a member of her family, in

7
00:00:49.878 --> 00:00:51.359
the hall of the Gorbeau house.

8
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.782
He could hear their voices through the thin wall that separated their apartment from his.

9
00:00:57.243 --> 00:00:58.964
But he had never paid attention.

10
00:00:59.552 --> 00:01:02.553
Yes, he had paid their rent, but anyone would have done that.

11
00:01:03.274 --> 00:01:06.615
A better neighbor would have paid attention to them and done more for them.

12
00:01:07.596 --> 00:01:11.737
Suddenly Marius noticed a small hole that he had never seen before.

13
00:01:12.418 --> 00:01:15.499
It was near the ceiling on the wall that divided the two apartments.

14
00:01:16.899 --> 00:01:21.201
"Let's see what this Jondrette family is like," said Marius as he climbed on a table.

15
00:01:21.221 --> 00:01:24.863
"It's not wrong to spy on people if your intention is to help them."

16
00:01:25.783 --> 00:01:27.124
Marius looked through the hole.

17
00:01:27.772 --> 00:01:31.054
His room was badly furnished, but at least it was clean.

18
00:01:31.994 --> 00:01:33.795
The room next door was filthy.

19
00:01:34.356 --> 00:01:40.319
The furniture consisted of a cane chair, a rickety table, and two dirty beds on either side of

20
00:01:40.339 --> 00:01:41.160
the fireplace.

21
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:47.503
The dark corners of this room hid who knows what—perhaps spiders as big as one's fist.

22
00:01:48.704 --> 00:01:51.785
A man with a long gray beard sat smoking at a table.

23
00:01:52.326 --> 00:01:55.948
He was writing, probably a letter such as the ones Marius had read.

24
00:01:56.872 --> 00:02:00.295
He was wearing a woman's blouse over a pair of muddy trousers.

25
00:02:00.916 --> 00:02:03.518
His toes stuck out from his broken-down boots.

26
00:02:04.519 --> 00:02:08.182
A large woman with graying red hair squatted near the fireplace.

27
00:02:09.303 --> 00:02:13.406
She too was wearing a blouse, but hers was worn with a patched skirt.

28
00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:17.189
Marius could see a girl lying listlessly on one of the beds.

29
00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.453
Probably the younger sister of the girl who had visited him.

30
00:02:22.513 --> 00:02:27.697
Marius was about to step away from his spy hole when the door to the neighbors' apartment opened

31
00:02:28.117 --> 00:02:29.577
and the elder daughter appeared.

32
00:02:30.498 --> 00:02:34.239
Wrapped in a ragged old gown, she was wearing men's work boots.

33
00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:38.720
She was breathless and yet there was a triumphant look on her face.

34
00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.901
"He's coming!"

35
00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:41.981
she announced.

36
00:02:42.001 --> 00:02:45.982
"I found him at the Church of Saint Jacques du Haut Pas."

37
00:02:46.002 --> 00:02:47.823
"The generous man?"

38
00:02:48.143 --> 00:02:49.423
asked Monsieur Jondrette.

39
00:02:50.484 --> 00:02:56.275
"Yes, he said his daughter had to make some purchases, but he would be along soon.

40
00:02:56.956 --> 00:02:58.557
I tell you he's coming now."

41
00:02:58.577 --> 00:03:00.999
"Wife, put out the fire!"

42
00:03:01.619 --> 00:03:07.083
The surprised woman did not stir, so her husband grabbed a broken pot on the mantel and threw

43
00:03:07.183 --> 00:03:08.124
water on the fire.

44
00:03:09.004 --> 00:03:13.207
Then, turning to his elder daughter, he said, "Break the chair!"

45
00:03:13.227 --> 00:03:15.769
"What do you mean?"

46
00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:16.610
she asked.

47
00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.572
He seized the chair and kicked out the cane seat.

48
00:03:20.244 --> 00:03:21.244
"Is it cold outside?"

49
00:03:21.504 --> 00:03:22.065
he asked her.

50
00:03:22.085 --> 00:03:25.066
"Very cold and it's snowing."

51
00:03:26.026 --> 00:03:28.307
The father turned toward the younger girl on the bed.

52
00:03:28.327 --> 00:03:31.568
"Get up, you good-for-nothing, and break a windowpane."

53
00:03:32.568 --> 00:03:34.689
The girl sprang off the bed, trembling.

54
00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:37.130
She was speechless at his command.

55
00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.290
"Did you hear me?"

56
00:03:39.731 --> 00:03:40.931
roared Monsieur Jondrette.

57
00:03:40.951 --> 00:03:44.640
"I told you to break a windowpane."

58
00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:50.222
The girl, with a sort of terrified obedience, rose up on tiptoe and smashed the window with her

59
00:03:50.383 --> 00:03:53.564
fist. The glass broke and fell with a crash.

60
00:03:53.584 --> 00:03:56.005
"Good," said the father.

61
00:03:56.725 --> 00:03:59.546
He ran his eyes over all the nooks and crannies of the room.

62
00:04:00.347 --> 00:04:03.988
He was like a general making his final preparations before the battle.

63
00:04:04.889 --> 00:04:10.511
Madame Jondrette slowly got up and asked, "Dear, what do you want me to do?"

64
00:04:10.531 --> 00:04:12.652
"Get into bed."

65
00:04:13.492 --> 00:04:16.694
His tone of voice showed he tolerated no disobedience.

66
00:04:17.235 --> 00:04:19.636
The mother threw herself heavily onto a bed.

67
00:04:20.537 --> 00:04:22.919
Meanwhile the younger daughter was sobbing.

68
00:04:23.559 --> 00:04:24.740
"What's wrong with you?"

69
00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:25.941
asked the father.

70
00:04:26.922 --> 00:04:28.563
She showed him her bleeding fist.

71
00:04:29.203 --> 00:04:31.685
In breaking the window, she had cut herself.

72
00:04:32.786 --> 00:04:35.127
It was the mother's turn to scold her husband.

73
00:04:35.147 --> 00:04:37.489
"See what a stupid thing you've done!

74
00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.332
You told her to break the glass, and she's hurt herself."

75
00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.620
"So much the better!

76
00:04:44.081 --> 00:04:45.901
Now she looks even more pitiful."

77
00:04:46.942 --> 00:04:51.303
Monsieur Jondrette tore a strip from his blouse and wound it around his daughter's hand.

78
00:04:52.163 --> 00:04:54.624
An icy wind blew through the broken window.

79
00:04:55.124 --> 00:04:58.165
The father looked around to be sure he hadn't forgotten anything.

80
00:04:58.185 --> 00:05:03.287
"I think we're ready to receive the generous man from the church."

81
00:05:04.887 --> 00:05:06.848
Just then there was a knock at the door.

82
00:05:07.444 --> 00:05:12.225
Monsieur Jondrette rushed forward and opened it with many low bows and smiles.

83
00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:15.386
"Ah, my benefactor!"

84
00:05:15.726 --> 00:05:16.566
he exclaimed.

85
00:05:16.586 --> 00:05:20.648
"Please come in and bring your lovely daughter with you."

86
00:05:21.608 --> 00:05:24.409
A man with white hair and a girl walked into the room.

87
00:05:25.069 --> 00:05:29.970
Marius, who had been watching all this, almost fell from his perch in surprise.

88
00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:36.852
It was Ursula—the radiant young woman who had been the object of his adoration in the Luxembourg Gardens!

89
00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.901
She looked just the same, perhaps a little paler.

90
00:05:41.522 --> 00:05:45.443
She was wearing a black cloak trimmed with fur and a violet velvet hat.

91
00:05:46.424 --> 00:05:51.106
Below her long dress, Marius caught a glimpse of her tiny foot in a satin shoe.

92
00:05:51.866 --> 00:05:58.269
The elder Jondrette daughter was also taking in the girl's outfit and her charming, happy face, but with

93
00:05:58.309 --> 00:05:59.850
a look of evil intent.

94
00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.071
The girl laid a large package on the table.

95
00:06:03.091 --> 00:06:10.916
"Monsieur, you'll find here some new clothes, warm stockings, and blankets," said Monsieur Leblanc.

96
00:06:10.936 --> 00:06:17.301
"Our angelic benefactor overwhelms us," Monsieur Jondrette said loudly.

97
00:06:18.141 --> 00:06:22.504
To his older daughter he whispered, "I don't want clothes and blankets!

98
00:06:23.265 --> 00:06:24.066
I want money!

99
00:06:24.646 --> 00:06:25.807
Which letter did you give him?"

100
00:06:25.827 --> 00:06:29.830
"The one signed Fabantou," she whispered back.

101
00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:31.931
"Right, the actor.

102
00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:38.231
Good." "I see that you are indeed to be pitied," said Monsieur Leblanc, looking around the room.

103
00:06:38.251 --> 00:06:40.072
"Yes, look.

104
00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:43.415
My poor darlings have no bread and no fire.

105
00:06:44.055 --> 00:06:46.897
The window is broken and in such weather as this!

106
00:06:48.118 --> 00:06:50.740
My wife is sick, and my child is injured.

107
00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.421
Look at her bleeding wrist.

108
00:06:53.582 --> 00:06:57.164
It was an accident at the factory where she earns only a few coins a week.

109
00:06:57.664 --> 00:07:02.828
I fear the doctor may have to cut off her arm—if we can even afford a doctor."

110
00:07:04.168 --> 00:07:08.170
"Indeed!" said Monsieur Leblanc, clearly alarmed by the situation.

111
00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.692
The girl, who believed that she might now lose her arm, began to sob.

112
00:07:13.172 --> 00:07:14.893
But her father paid no attention.

113
00:07:15.793 --> 00:07:21.835
He was busy looking at the generous man because there was something familiar about that man.

114
00:07:21.855 --> 00:07:26.357
"The rent is due in a few days," Jondrette went on.

115
00:07:26.377 --> 00:07:28.578
"I owe sixty francs."

116
00:07:29.679 --> 00:07:32.400
Monsieur Leblanc drew five francs from his pocket.

117
00:07:33.032 --> 00:07:34.694
"That's all I have right now.

118
00:07:35.514 --> 00:07:38.997
I must take my daughter home, but I'll bring you sixty francs tonight.

119
00:07:39.597 --> 00:07:41.559
And here—take my coat."

120
00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.622
Monsieur Jondrette escorted the Leblancs downstairs.

121
00:07:46.283 --> 00:07:50.846
Marius was desperate to learn where they lived, but he couldn't let the father catch sight of him.

122
00:07:51.667 --> 00:07:54.649
He followed them downstairs and saw them getting into a cab.

123
00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:58.573
Marius looked frantically for a cab but couldn't find one.

124
00:07:59.273 --> 00:08:04.224
As their cab disappeared around the corner, he stood in the street and watched helplessly.
